{"symbol":"AKT2","resolved":{"input":"AKT2","symbol":"AKT2","ncbi_gene_id":"208","name":"AKT serine/threonine kinase 2","location":"19q13.2","aliases":["HIHGHH","PKBB","PKBBETA","PRKBB","RAC-BETA"],"uniprot":"P31751","uniprot_reviewed":true},"function":[{"text":"AKT2 function (UniProt P31751, UniProt evidence code ECO:0000269): Serine/threonine kinase closely related to AKT1 and AKT3. All 3 enzymes, AKT1, AKT2 and AKT3, are collectively known as AKT kinase. AKT regulates many processes including metabolism, proliferation, cell survival, growth and angiogenesis, through the phosphorylation of a range of downstream substrates. Over 100 substrates have been reported so far, although for most of them, the precise AKT kinase catalyzing the reaction was not specified. AKT regulates glucose uptake by mediating insulin-induced translocation of the SLC2A4/GLUT4 glucose transporter to the cell surface. Phosphorylation of PTPN1 at 'Ser-50' negatively modulates its phosphatase activity preventing dephosphorylation of the ...","evidence":"experimentally validated","code":"EXP","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P31751","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P31751/entry","title":"AKT2: RAC-beta serine/threonine-protein kinase"},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:23444369","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23444369/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:31204173","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31204173/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:21432781","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21432781/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:21620960","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21620960/","title":""}]},{"text":"AKT2 function (UniProt P31751, UniProt evidence code ECO:0000250): Several AKT2-specific substrates have been identified, including ANKRD2, C2CD5, CLK2 and PITX2. May play a role in myoblast differentiation. In this context, may act through PITX2 phosphorylation. Unphosphorylated PITX2 associates with an ELAVL1/HuR-containing complex, which stabilizes CCND1 cyclin mRNA, ensuring cell proliferation. Phosphorylation by AKT2 impairs this association, leading to CCND1 mRNA destabilization and progression towards differentiation. Also involved in the negative regulation of myogenesis in response to stress conditions. In this context, acts by phosphorylating ANKRD2. May also be a key regulator of glucose uptake. Regulates insulin-stimulated glucose transport by ...","evidence":"computationally predicted","code":"PRED","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P31751","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P31751/entry","title":"AKT2: RAC-beta serine/threonine-protein kinase"}]}],"go":[{"text":"AKT2 GO biological process annotations: cellular response to insulin stimulus, insulin receptor signaling pathway, negative regulation of long-chain fatty acid import across plasma membrane, positive regulation of cap-dependent translational initiation, positive regulation of cell migration, positive regulation of cell motility, positive regulation of D-glucose import across plasma membrane, positive regulation of fatty acid beta-oxidation, positive regulation of glucose metabolic process, positive regulation of glycogen biosynthetic process, protein stabilization","evidence":"experimentally validated","code":"EXP","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P31751","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P31751/entry","title":"AKT2: RAC-beta serine/threonine-protein kinase"}]},{"text":"AKT2 GO biological process annotations: fat cell differentiation, mammary gland epithelial cell differentiation, protein modification process, regulation of cell cycle, regulation of cell migration, signal transduction","evidence":"curated database evidence","code":"CUR","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P31751","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P31751/entry","title":"AKT2: RAC-beta serine/threonine-protein kinase"}]},{"text":"AKT2 GO biological process annotations: intracellular signal transduction, negative regulation of apo